---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { repoUrl } from '@/lib/buildprints';
const submitIssueUrl = `${repoUrl}/issues/new?title=${encodeURIComponent('[submission] <your buildprint title>')}&body=${encodeURIComponent(`Submit a free Buildprint for review.\n\nRepository URL:\nPackage path, if not repo root:\nCreator name/profile:\nBuildprint title:\nCategory: Product OS / Feature / Extension / Framework / Architecture / Workflow OS\nLicense:\n\nWhat it helps people build:\n\nVerification checklist:\n- [ ] BUILDPRINT.md exists\n- [ ] SPEC.md or equivalent behavior spec exists, if applicable\n- [ ] PLAN.md or phased steps exist\n- [ ] TEST_MATRIX.md or quality checks exist\n- [ ] No secrets, private keys, tokens, or env values are included\n- [ ] Links resolve\n- [ ] License is clear\n\nNotes for reviewer:\n`)}`;
const creatorPrompt = `You are helping me turn an existing software project into an Agent Buildprint: an expert blueprint that humans can review and AI coding agents can follow.

Use a two-stage process. Do not modify application code. Do not copy secrets, tokens, private keys, .env values, credentials, customer data, or private production URLs. If you see env vars, record only variable names and purpose.

STAGE 1 — Discover candidates first
Inspect the repo and create discovery outputs only:
- SYSTEM_MAP.md — architecture zones, key flows, integrations, data stores, side effects, tests, unknowns
- BUILDPRINT_CANDIDATES.md — 3-7 possible reusable Buildprints with title, scope, included paths, excluded paths, why reusable, risks, questions, estimated tier
- questions.md — human decisions needed

If the project is large, stop after Stage 1 and ask me which candidate to extract, or whether to create a full hierarchical System Buildprint. Do not flatten the whole repo into one vague document.

STAGE 2 — Extract only the chosen scope
After I choose a candidate or provide a clear scope, create a clean package in buildprint-submission/. If I explicitly choose full-system mode, create project.buildprint/ with modules/.

For a normal scoped Buildprint, create:
- BUILDPRINT.md — architecture, purpose, intended build outcome, non-goals
- SPEC.md — behavior requirements, must/must-not rules, edge cases
- PLAN.md — implementation phases another agent could follow
- CONTRACTS.md — interfaces, data models, APIs, integrations, side effects
- TEST_MATRIX.md — quality gates, acceptance checks, risks mapped to tests
- VALIDATION_TEMPLATE.md — honest final report template
- questions.md — unknowns that require human/creator review
- README.md — marketplace-facing overview: what this helps people build, who it is for, stack, license, and usage notes
- SUBMISSION_CHECKLIST.md — files created, scope, included/excluded paths, known gaps, commands run, secrets check result, and estimated tier

Output rules:
1. Label important claims as OBSERVED, INFERRED, or QUESTION.
2. Do not invent validation results.
3. Do not claim tests pass unless you actually ran them.
4. Prefer concise, practical Markdown over long essays.
5. Make the Buildprint useful for Codex, Cursor, Claude Code, or other coding agents.
6. Mark unrelated systems as OUT_OF_SCOPE for the selected Buildprint.

Start with Stage 1 now.`;
---
<BaseLayout title="Submit a Buildprint — Agent Buildprint" description="Submit a free expert Buildprint for review and validation.">
  <main>
    <section class="container hero compact-hero">
      <a class="pill" href="/buildprints/">← Registry</a>
      <h1>Submit a free Buildprint.</h1>
      <p class="lead">Creators can submit expert blueprints for products, features, architectures, workflows, and integrations. Submissions are verified before they appear in the registry.</p>
      <div class="actions"><a class="btn primary" href={submitIssueUrl}>Submit for review</a><a class="btn" href="/buildprints/buildprint-mapper-os/">Mapper OS</a><a class="btn" href="https://github.com/DomEscobar/agent-buildprint">Open GitHub</a></div>
    </section>

    <section class="container section submission-prompt-section">
      <div class="panel code-card prompt-card">
        <div class="panel-row"><div><span class="eyebrow"><b>✎</b> Creator prompt</span><h2>Turn your project into scoped Buildprint candidates.</h2></div><button class="btn copy-btn" data-copy={creatorPrompt}>Copy prompt</button></div>
        <p>Paste this into Codex, Cursor, Claude Code, or another coding agent inside your repo. Large projects should first produce candidate Buildprints and ask for scope before final extraction.</p>
        <code class="code-block-text">{creatorPrompt}</code>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="container section submission-flow">
      <div class="section-head simple"><div><span class="eyebrow"><b>✓</b> Verification</span><h2>GitHub is the first identity and review layer.</h2><p>No marketplace auth yet. For now, creator identity comes from the GitHub account/repo that submits the package, and every Buildprint is reviewed before listing.</p></div></div>
      <div class="grid how-grid">
        <div class="panel quiet"><span class="step-number">1</span><h3>Map or discover scope</h3><p>For large repos, use Mapper OS or the creator prompt to produce candidates before extraction. No CLI is required.</p></div>
        <div class="panel quiet"><span class="step-number">2</span><h3>Extract selected Buildprint</h3><p>Create one scoped package, or a hierarchical System Buildprint with modules. Avoid whole-repo sludge.</p></div>
        <div class="panel quiet"><span class="step-number">3</span><h3>Submit for review</h3><p>Open a GitHub issue with repo URL, package path, creator profile, license, scope, and checklist.</p></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="container section grid columns">
      <div class="panel code-card">
        <div class="panel-row"><span class="eyebrow"><b>▦</b> Expected folder</span></div>
        <code class="code-block-text">my-buildprint/
  BUILDPRINT.md          # architecture / intent
  SPEC.md                # behavior, if applicable
  PLAN.md                # execution plan
  CONTRACTS.md           # interfaces/data contracts
  TEST_MATRIX.md         # checks and acceptance gates
  VALIDATION_TEMPLATE.md # honest completion report
  prompts/
  policies/
  tests/
  examples/</code>
      </div>
      <div class="panel">
        <div class="panel-row"><span class="eyebrow"><b>✓</b> Review checks</span></div>
        <ul class="clean-list check-list">
          <li>Package has a clear build outcome</li>
          <li>Required files are present for its tier</li>
          <li>No secrets, tokens, or private data</li>
          <li>Links and package manifest resolve</li>
          <li>Quality gates are concrete, not vibes</li>
          <li>License and creator/source are clear</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="panel">
        <div class="panel-row"><span class="eyebrow"><b>★</b> Stars for demand</span></div>
        <p>Stars and requests use GitHub first. That keeps early demand signals tied to real accounts without building a full auth system yet.</p>
        <div class="chip-list">
          <span><b>★</b> Star/request</span>
          <span><b>↗</b> GitHub identity</span>
          <span><b>✓</b> review queue</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
  <script>
    function copyText(value) {
      if (navigator.clipboard?.writeText) return navigator.clipboard.writeText(value);
      const textarea = document.createElement('textarea');
      textarea.value = value;
      textarea.setAttribute('readonly', '');
      textarea.style.position = 'fixed';
      textarea.style.opacity = '0';
      document.body.appendChild(textarea);
      textarea.select();
      document.execCommand('copy');
      textarea.remove();
      return Promise.resolve();
    }

    document.querySelectorAll('.copy-btn').forEach((button) => {
      button.setAttribute('data-label', button.textContent || 'Copy');
      button.addEventListener('click', () => {
        const value = button.getAttribute('data-copy') || '';
        const original = button.getAttribute('data-label') || 'Copy';
        button.textContent = 'Copied ✓';
        button.classList.add('copied');
        button.setAttribute('aria-live', 'polite');
        copyText(value).catch(() => {
          button.textContent = 'Copy failed';
        });
        setTimeout(() => {
          button.textContent = original;
          button.classList.remove('copied');
        }, 2200);
      });
    });
  </script>
</BaseLayout>
